message.content:
Based on my analysis of the panoramic radiograph, I identified periapical changes at four sites: the maxillary right canine/lateral apex, the maxillary left canine apex, and the apical regions of the mandibular posterior teeth bilaterally.

```json
{"lesions": [
  {"box": [0.265, 0.270, 0.320, 0.350], "pai": 4},
  {"box": [0.615, 0.290, 0.670, 0.370], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.315, 0.580, 0.365, 0.655], "pai": 3},
  {"box": [0.625, 0.585, 0.675, 0.655], "pai": 3}
]}
```

Caveat: panoramic radiographs have significant superimposition and geometric distortion, so periapical assessment here is limited compared with intraoral periapical films. The most convincing finding was the rounded radiolucency at the maxillary right canine apex (graded 4); the other three sites showed subtler apical bone change (graded 3). If you have intraoral periapical images of these teeth, they would confirm/refine these grades.
